# Missing Graph Selector Trial Run Notes

This review did not put the two terms in the same failure bucket. "missing graph selectors" points to graph selectors, while yanked advisory lint needs to be examined from the pre-call state; both can pass through the bans area, but the observations they leave are different. CDP-205 preserve the selector samples reported by the user, while CDP-221 continue tracking whether any visible change appears after the yanked case completes.

src/ban.rs is the starting point for this verification. The adjacency in the file header is enough to show that this area touches lint levels, error propagation, parallel processing, and version requirements at the same time:

```rust
use crate::LintLevel;
use failure::Error;
use rayon::prelude::*;
use semver::{Version, VersionReq};
```

When recording the sample, state first that the selector points to the graph root; do not write only "skip failed." The configuration key in the card is `bans.skip-tree`; it helps locate the input but cannot replace checking whether the graph root exists. The conclusion from the review is: A missing bans.skip-tree root does not panic or index past the graph; normal bans analysis continues with a grouped duplicate diagnostic. Here, grouped duplicate diagnostic means that duplicates are reported by group rather than analysis stopping after an out-of-bounds index.

This result also narrows the scope of the yanked item. What has not been observed is not another graph-root error, but an additional visible change after advisory lint completes; until that phenomenon occurs, do not write it into selector behavior. If subsequent samples show both types of information, first save the selector input and the pre-call advisory state separately, then compare the final diagnostics.

This page retains only the entry point and observations from the current review. The next experiment should change only the pre-call state of yanked advisory lint; keep the missing-root sample unchanged and focus on whether new output appears rather than repeating the graph-root branch check.

# Initial Configuration and Default License Decision

The first draft covered only two names that are easy to group together: `initial configuration creation` and `default license behavior`. At the time, there wasn't enough material to establish that they shared an implementation location, a source of defaults, or a maintenance boundary.

Two approaches were considered. One was to merge the two names into a single CLI configuration rule; the other was to write the default license conclusion first and add the creation location later. Both would save space, but would treat entry-point clues as evidence of behavior. The decision was to keep two independent questions: for configuration creation, find the actual creation operation; for license behavior, find the default branch in policy evaluation.

Use `src/cargo-deny/main.rs` as a starting point for reading, not as the implementation location for either topic. The top of the file shows:

```rust
use ansi_term::Color;
use cargo_deny::licenses;
use failure::{bail, format_err, Error};
use std::path::PathBuf;
```

These imports are enough to show that CLI output, error objects, path values, and `cargo_deny::licenses` appear near the same entry point. On their own, they do not establish where the configuration object is created or what the default license value is. If later evidence shows only proximity to the entry point, keep it recorded as an entry-point observation; update the corresponding topic only after finding the creation operation or the actual evaluation branch.
